  "ch7.esp": "LBA {s} to {e}",
  "ch7.esp.none": "None",
  "ch7.why.sep": ", ",
  "ch7.why.sig": "signature wrong",
  "ch7.why.hdrcrc": "header CRC mismatch",
  "ch7.why.mylba": "MyLBA is not this sector",
  "ch7.why.arrcrc": "partition entry CRC mismatch",
  "ch7.say.primary": "The primary header's signature, CRC32, MyLBA and the partition entries' CRC32 all check out. The firmware reads {n} partitions, and the EFI system partition starts at LBA {s}; it looks there for the boot loader.",
  "ch7.say.backup": "The primary header failed its check ({why}), so the firmware switches to the backup header at the last sector of the disk, LBA {last}. It is intact, and the ESP is still found. The reference implementation, edk2, also writes the primary header back from the backup at this point.",
  "ch7.say.none": "Both the primary header ({why}) and the backup are broken, so the firmware finds no partitions at all, and as far as it is concerned this disk cannot boot.",
  "ch7.say.fixed": "The bytes were changed and the CRC32 was recomputed to match, so the firmware accepts the header: what it reads has been altered, and it treats it as good. CRC32 detects only accidental corruption; anyone who changes the data on purpose can compute a matching value easily.",
  "ch7.p4": "On the default 64 GiB disk, the header's CRC32 is {gCrc} and the partition entry array's is {gArrCrc}; the last sector is LBA {gLast}, where the backup header sits; the ESP starts at LBA {gEsp} (1 MiB aligned). Change any one byte at random and the CRC no longer matches, so the firmware switches to the backup; change both copies and the disk cannot be found. But with “recompute CRC after editing” ticked, almost every change gets past the check, except fields that have their own checks, such as the signature and MyLBA. <strong>CRC guards against disk errors, not against tampering</strong>: to tell whether a file has been deliberately swapped, you need a key that only the other side holds. That is the next chapter.",
  "ch7.p5": "On an old BIOS, the code that runs at boot is the code in the first 446 bytes of the MBR, which then looks for the next stage in a partition. UEFI does not run any “sector” on the disk. It understands the FAT file system itself, and opens a .efi file directly by path from the ESP, such as Windows' \\EFI\\Microsoft\\Boot\\bootmgfw.efi, or the shimx64.efi of a Linux distribution.",
  "ch7.limit": "The model builds only two partitions. It checks the items the UEFI specification requires in the header (signature, header CRC, MyLBA, partition entry CRC), and it also checks the 0xEE of the protective MBR. A real implementation also checks whether partitions run past the end of the disk or overlap each other, and the numbers differ on disks with 4096-byte sectors.",

  "ch8.title": "Each stage verifies the next: Secure Boot",
  "ch8.law": "Secure Boot has each stage verify the signature of the next before it hands over control: the firmware verifies the boot loader, and the boot loader verifies the kernel. What is checked is whether the hash of this file can be matched to its signature with a trusted public key. If the signature does not match, if the signer is not on the trusted list, or if the file is on the blocklist, it does not run.",
  "ch8.p1": "A parcel locker does not know you; it checks only the pickup code, and if the code matches, the door opens. A signature is like a pickup code that only the sender can generate but anyone can check. Whoever checks it does not need to know how it was made, only the public half of the key the sender has published.",
  "ch8.p2": "This happens in two steps. First, compute the file's <strong>hash</strong>: SHA-256 compresses a file of any length into 32 bytes; change one bit and the result is completely different, and no one can make another file with the same hash. Then verify the <strong>signature</strong>: the signer uses a private key d, which only they know, to turn the hash h into s = h<sup>d</sup> mod n. The verifier uses the public (n, e) to compute s<sup>e</sup> mod n and checks whether it equals h. Without d you cannot produce an s that passes, so a file that passes was signed by the holder of the private key and not one byte has changed. This is RSA signing. For why reversing it is hard, see <a href='../rsa-encryption/'>“RSA Public-Key Encryption”</a>. The essay still in progress, “A Secret Agreed in Public”, will tell the whole story of where public keys come from.",
  "ch8.p3": "The firmware holds four sets of keys, all stored in NVRAM variables. <strong>PK</strong> (Platform Key, usually belonging to the motherboard maker) decides who can change the KEK. The <strong>KEK</strong> (Key Exchange Key) decides who can update db and dbx. <strong>db</strong> lists the allowed signers and hashes. <strong>dbx</strong> lists the forbidden ones; Microsoft's notes state that when a file is checked, dbx must be consulted first, and a match means the file may not run. The certificates Microsoft placed in db fall into two groups: those for Windows itself (Windows Production PCA 2011, now replaced by Windows UEFI CA 2023), and those for third parties (Microsoft UEFI CA 2011, now split into Microsoft UEFI CA 2023 and Option ROM UEFI CA 2023). Linux distributions work this way: they have Microsoft sign a small shim with a third-party certificate; the shim carries the distribution's own certificate and uses it to verify GRUB and the kernel. The machine's owner can also enroll their own key through MOK (Machine Owner Key).",
  "ch8.p4": "The model below really computes. Every file gets a real SHA-256, and the signatures use the real RSA formula, but the modulus is only about 62 bits (a real one is 2048 bits). The checks run in the same order as the firmware: first dbx, then whether the signer is on the trusted list, then the signature. The shim also checks the SBAT generation at the end (next chapter).",
  "ch8.model.title": "A chain of trust: swap one link and see where it breaks",
  "ch8.model.how": "Pick a file for each stage: the genuine one, one with a byte changed, an old version with a known flaw, or one you built yourself. Turn Secure Boot on or off, update dbx, enroll your MOK, and see at which stage verification stops, and why.",
